fp->f_ci->m_fattr = (sparse->SetSparse) ? (fp->f_ci->m_fattr | FILE_ATTRIBUTE_SPARSE_FILE_LE) : (fp->f_ci->m_fattr & ~FILE_ATTRIBUTE_SPARSE_FILE_LE);
Reason:
The function modifies the file's sparse attribute and saves it via xattr without any prior permission checks. This allows a client on a read‑only share to change the attribute, and clients lacking FILE_WRITE_DATA or FILE_WRITE_ATTRIBUTES access to do so on writable shares, because neither share‑level nor handle‑level access control is enforced before the modification.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!test_tree_conn_flag(work->tcon, KSMBD_TREE_CONN_FLAG_WRITABLE)) {
    ksmbd_debug(SMB, "User does not have write permission\n");
    return -EACCES;
}
if (!(fp->daccess & (FILE_WRITE_DATA_LE | FILE_WRITE_ATTRIBUTES_LE))) {
    ret = -EACCES;
    goto out;
}
Reason:
The first added check verifies that the tree connection is writable, preventing clients on read‑only shares from modifying the sparse attribute. The second check ensures that the open handle grants either FILE_WRITE_DATA or FILE_WRITE_ATTRIBUTES access, preventing unauthorized per‑handle modifications. Both checks are placed before the attribute change, and upon failure the function returns an error (or jumps to the cleanup label), avoiding unintended side effects.

